WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.580
So let's start with caramelization.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.140
On a famous desert, Crema Catalana.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.780
Here we have the cream already made and cooled down

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.480
with that think skin that forms on the top and that will help us to add the sugar

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.040
to make that crystal that we want to make

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.960
all at once ... we need to be generous.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.700
but it is important to spread it evenly

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.600
in order to be able to apply the iron.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.720
The iron needs to be red hot

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.000
at a very high temperature.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.160
So we apply the iron on top of the cream

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.240
To achieve that burnt aspect, giving it that effect.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.400
Obviously right now it'd still be soft

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.300
but it hardens instantaneously.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.680
If you want we could show how it breaks.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.400
What do you think?

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.320
Here's that instantaneous caramelization effect,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.260
to yield that sound ...

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.960
Aah ... it cracks.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.260
That crystal, that sugar crystal.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.640
Now go ahead and taste it.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.240
OK, thank you.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.940
Mmmm ... I love Crema Catalana.  Thank you.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.100
Thank you.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.200
So caramelization is just what happens when you heat sugar,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.820
white table sugar.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.320
So sugar is just a sucrose molecule

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.940
and sucrose is made of fructose and glucose.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.760
And so the first thing that happens when we apply heat

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.920
is that these two molecules fall apart

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.300
into glucose and fructose.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.680
And so here is what these ring structures would look like.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.960
And then these ring structures just sort of explode

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.500
into a wide array of other small molecules.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.500
And all of these molecules have different flavors

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.540
and different smells.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.020
And typically in caramelization,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.780
the flavors that we're talking about

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.040
are these sort of sherry, buttery, nutty flavors.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.940
And this happens at temperatures of about 165 degrees Celsius or higher.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.920
So if we were to take these flavors

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.700
and put them on what we call a flavor wheel,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.980
which is what wine tasters

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.920
or tasters in general would use

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.280
when they would try to sort of guide their

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.620
pinpointing of what a flavor is,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:34.700
and here's a flavor wheel with all of the different flavors

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.100
everything from earthy to nutty to fruity to flowery

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.680
And so caramelization kind of creates

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.660
these particular flavors

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.840
that are sort of caramely and nutty-buttery.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.340
And so now keep this in mind

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.180
because now we're going to move on to Maillard reactions

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.180
and see what kinds of flavors those create.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.560
Maillard reactions, in contrast to caramelization reactions

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.400
involve the reactions between sugars and amino acids.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.340
And these typically take place at temperatures above 120 degrees Celsius.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.420
And so what happens here is that

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.180
if you for example just take milk

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.020
and heat it

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.120
There's going to be sugars in the milk and there's going to be

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.600
proteins in the milk

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.700
amino acids

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.640
And the sugars are going to react with the amino acids

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.980
And create these sort of browner compounds

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.300
that have nutty flavors

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.940
And so you've probably had this

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.920
If you've ever had dulce de leche

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.900
you've had this exact same thing

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.800
and there are a lot of desserts and a lot of sauces that build on this concept

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.200
of just heating milk

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.660
and creating these flavors.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.520
And so there are many different kinds of amino acids

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.100
There's about 20 amino acids

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.480
And each of them, the first step

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.200
would be that the glucose

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.340
reacts with the amino group

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.280
on any of the amino acids

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.600
And so the amino group, it is a nucleophile

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.140
if you think back to chemistry

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.020
And the nucleophile is going to attack the carbon

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.680
on the glucose, here.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.600
And this is then going to form a bond

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.140
And this is kind of the first step

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.660
in the Maillard reactions.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.580
And after this there is a whole

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.580
again, just an array of different reactions

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.620
that can happen

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.020
The molecule falls apart

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.000
it reacts again

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.560
it reacts with each other,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.520
with other glucoses, with other amino acids

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.920
but this is the first step.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.360
And so you can imagine then, that if there is

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.640
20 amino acids, and this is the first step

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:26.300
you can imagine that there is just a lot of different molecules that can be created

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.020
Even with this just being the first step.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.520
The characteristic feature of Maillard products

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.180
is that they often contain sulfur and nitrogen

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.340
which come from the amino acids in the protein.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.260
And sulfur in protein often contributes these kind of meaty

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.140
earthy flavors that we associate with when we cook a steak

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.200
and there's this sort of earthy, meaty flavor

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.620
and this also then contributes to a lot of the other sort of non-distinct flavors

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.860
that are very different from the flavors that we get in caramelization.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.680
Another feature that makes Maillard reactions special

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.500
and interesting from a chemical perspective

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.980
is that they're often exothermic reactions.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.140
Exothermic reactions produce heat as they happen.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.120
And so as Maillard reactions start to happen

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.120
they produce heat, and then this leads to more reactions happening.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.360
And so this speeds up.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.440
And so you may have experienced this, actually, if you have ever made toast.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.480
So sometimes you may have experienced that you make toast

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.100
and you wait, and wait, and it's not done, and it's not done,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.940
and then suddenly it's done and then it's done and then it's overdone

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.400
and they just happen very, very quickly.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.120
Millard reactions, of course, are not only in baking

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.120
but they are basically everywhere around us.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.860
Here are just some examples.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.340
Everything from coffee to popcorn to champagne

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:45.160
to a nice cured ham are all places where Maillard reactions have taken place

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.520
And in some of these cases there hasn't even been an addition of heat

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.440
So in champagne, for example

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.920
the reaction is allowed to take place over so long

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.540
that the reactions take place anyway, even though there is no input of heat.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.560
But all of these sort of earthy flavors that you get

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.080
in a champagne, or in meat

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.500
they're all also due to Maillard reactions.

